code:
fn longest(a: &str, b: &str) -> &str {
    if a.len() > b.len() { a } else { b }
}

reference:
{
  "false_carryover": {
    "what_a_source_dev_assumes": "this is fine \u2014 same as C returning one of two `const char *` pointers; the compiler trusts the programmer",
    "what_actually_happens": "compile error: 'missing lifetime specifier'. The compiler cannot infer how long the returned reference is valid. Must be written `fn longest<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str`"
  },
  "lens_must_say": [
    "Rust references carry compile-time lifetimes; when a function returns a reference, the compiler must be able to relate it to one of the inputs",
    "this code does not compile without a lifetime annotation: `fn longest<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str`",
    "the annotation tells the compiler the returned reference lives at least as long as both inputs",
    "C has no equivalent \u2014 the C compiler trusts the programmer to manage pointer lifetimes by hand"
  ],
  "lens_must_not_say": [
    "this compiles as written",
    "C-style pointer return is fine in Rust",
    "lifetimes are a runtime concept",
    "the annotation changes how the code runs"
  ],
  "expert_notes": "Lifetimes are the most foreign Rust concept for a C dev. The lens should frame them as 'the compiler asking you to write down what was previously in a comment or your head.' Bonus if it notes that lifetimes have zero runtime cost."
}

